latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Color Frequency Analysis:\n   a. Iterate through the input grid.\n   b. Count the frequency of each non-black color (any color except 0).\n   c. Store these counts in a dictionary or similar data structure.\n\n2. Color Selection:\n   a. Sort the colors by their frequency in descending order.\n   b. Select the top 4 most frequent colors (or fewer if there aren't 4 unique colors).\n\n3. Relative Frequency Calculation:\n   a. Identify the maximum frequency among the selected colors.\n   b. For each selected color, calculate its relative frequency by dividing its count by the maximum frequency.\n\n4. Output Grid Width Determination:\n   a. Sum up the relative frequencies of the selected colors.\n   b. Multiply this sum by 5.\n   c. Round the result to the nearest integer to get the output grid width.\n\n5. Output Grid Initialization:\n   a. Create a 4xN grid where N is the calculated width from step 4.\n   b. Initialize all cells with black (0).\n\n6. Color Placement:\n   a. Sort the selected colors by their frequency in descending order.\n   b. For each color, starting from the most frequent:\n      i. Calculate the number of columns it should occupy by multiplying its relative frequency by the grid width and rounding to the nearest integer.\n      ii. Fill in the corresponding row from right to left with this color, for the calculated number of columns.\n      iii. Move to the next row for the next color.\n\n7. Final Adjustment:\n   a. Ensure the most frequent color (top row) fills the entire width of the grid.\n   b. If any columns on the left remain unfilled (black), shift all color sections to the right to ensure right-alignment.\n\n8. Output Generation:\n   Create and return a ColoredGrid object with the values from the constructed grid.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects of the challenge:\n- It considers only non-black colors in the frequency analysis.\n- It limits the output to the top 4 most frequent colors.\n- It creates a 4-row output grid with variable width based on relative frequencies.\n- It ensures right-alignment of color sections.\n- It represents relative frequencies proportionally in the output.\n- It works for inputs with fewer than 4 unique colors.\n- The multiplication by 5 in step 4 provides a good balance between compactness and distinguishability.\n\nThis approach should generalize well across all provided examples and potential test cases, capturing the essence of the color distribution while maintaining a consistent output format."
